# When the {{ twin_name }} twin asks for a new model

The twin asks for this when a forecast question stayed outside its tolerance after it re-fitted its model{% if fidelity.reselect %} and tried every candidate of the specification{% endif %}: what it can do by itself did not hold. You see it as an incident in status `remodel_requested`, or as the notification `twin.fidelity.remodel_requested`. The twin waits {{ fidelity.remodel_timeout }} from the request (`requested_at`); then the incident is `failed`, and so is the twin.

Questions this can concern:

| Question | Horizon | Tolerance | Model specification |
|---|---|---|---|
{% for q in fidelity.scored %}
| `{{ q.id }}` | {{ q.horizon or "-" }} | {{ q.tolerance or "-" }} | `{{ q.model }}` |
{% endfor %}

The work is done in the twin's workspace, with `dta`, never through the API: the twin's model specification, features and code live in the manifest and the workspace, and a build brings the twin in line with them.

## 1. Read what the twin found

With the helpers of `reference/recipes.md` (Setup):

```python
asked = get("/fidelity/incidents", status="remodel_requested").json()["data"]
incident = get(f"/fidelity/incidents/{asked[0]['uid']}").json()["data"]
print(incident["subject"], incident["opened_at"], incident["requested_at"], incident["detail"])
for step in incident["steps"]:                   # every correction tried, and what it gave
    print(step)
```

Or `dta fidelity incidents --status remodel_requested` and `dta fidelity incident <uid>`.

Then the question's errors and the backtests:

- `GET /fidelity/questions/{id}/errors` (`dta fidelity errors <question>`): the error per forecast origin. When did it rise: at once (something changed at a moment) or slowly (a drift)?
- `GET /models?question=<question>` and `GET /models/{uid}/backtests`: the backtests of the build and those of the corrections (scope `recent`). A model that was accepted on the old history and not on the recent one fits a system that no longer exists.
- `GET /fidelity/signals`: a suspect sensor can look like a bad model.

## 2. Find what changed in the real system

A model that kept its tolerance for a long time and then lost it usually describes the system as it was before something changed. Look for the change, at the time the error rose:

- `GET /history/diff` and `GET /objects/{type}/{uid}/history` around that time: equipment added or taken out, a setting changed, a new object.
- `GET /sync/sources` and `GET /sync/runs`: a source that changed what it sends, rows set aside, a gap.
{% if actions %}
- `GET /actions/records?status=executed`: what the twin itself did to the system in that time.
{% endif %}
- Ask the user: work on site, a new operating regime, a season, a sensor recalibrated.

Name the change before you revise anything. If you find none, say so: a revision without a cause is a guess.

## 3. Revise the model in the workspace

Change what the change calls for, and nothing else:

- the model specification in `manifest.yaml` (`models.<spec>`): its candidates, their family and parameters, its features, its training window;
- the features the model uses, or the code of the twin's own models and functions in the workspace.

Do not change the question, its tolerance or its horizon to make the error fit: those are the contract, agreed with the user. If the contract no longer fits the real system, tell the user; that is a revision of the contract, which the user agrees and signs.

## 4. Validate, sign only if the contract changed, build

```sh
dta manifest validate        # must pass
dta build                    # registers and evaluates the revised specification
```

- A change outside `contract` (models, features, functions, code) needs no new signature: validate, then build.
- If the contract changed after all, the build refuses until it is signed again: the user confirms the fields that changed (`dta manifest confirm <field>…`), you validate again, and the user signs (`dta manifest sign`). The signature is the user's, never yours.
- The build evaluates every specification whose candidates it registered anew or changed. Read its `models` step: a revised specification with no accepted candidate is not better on the twin's own history; go back to 2.

## 5. Let the twin judge it, and check the incident

The twin evaluates again by itself: at its next check it sees a model of the specification registered after the request, goes back to choosing among the candidates on recent data, makes the best accepted one active and waits for at least 3 forecasts it has not seen to come true within the tolerance. Do not start evaluations or corrections by hand to hurry it.

```sh
dta fidelity incident <uid>   # rung, awaiting, steps; resolved once the evidence is in
dta fidelity status
```

Each check is {{ fidelity.interval }} apart and each question needs 3 origins after the model's data ends, so judging takes at least 3 forecasts' time. Tell the user what you changed and why, and that the twin is judging it.

## 6. When it cannot be done in time

If you cannot finish before `requested_at` + {{ fidelity.remodel_timeout }}, when the incident becomes `failed` (the cause is not found, the data that would show it is missing, a decision or access only the user has is needed), tell the user before the time is up, plainly:

- which question is outside its tolerance, since when, and by how much (window error against tolerance);
- what the twin tried, and what you found so far;
- what is missing, and who can provide it;
- that when the time is up the twin becomes `failed`: its answers are then to be reported as unreliable{% if executes %} and it executes no action{% endif %}, until a model fitted after the failure keeps the tolerance for 3 forecasts, or the user asks for `dta fidelity retry <uid>` once the cause is mended.

Never resolve the incident, raise the tolerance or disable the question to avoid the failure.
